llvm-3.3 backport patch: a loop whose latch was just folded is not rotated (PS4)

This patch CHANGES CODE GENERATION for a loop whose latch LoopRotate's
simplifyLoopLatch folds into the final exiting block (a scan such as
`while (i < n && buf[i] != c) i++;`): the loop is left unrotated, the header
test first and the increment in the body, as LLVM did before

  r181230  9c72b071fe4e  2013-05-06  Andrew Trick, "Rotate multi-exit loops
           even if the latch was simplified." (fixes PR10293)

which 3.3 (branched 2013-05-07) carries. The patch takes the SimplifiedLatch
parameter out of rotateLoop's exiting-latch test, restoring r181230's
pre-state for that condition and nothing else (the latch fold itself, the
repeat loop in runOnLoop and the latch's return value stay as 3.3 has them).
Measured over every claimed library row: no claimed function changes;
0x1a0780 matches under it and 0x19da90's loops take the base's shape.
docs/DECISIONS.md D36.

--- a/lib/Transforms/Scalar/LoopRotation.cpp
+++ b/lib/Transforms/Scalar/LoopRotation.cpp
@@ -290,9 +290,11 @@
   if (OrigLatch == 0)
     return false;
 
-  // Rotate if either the loop latch does *not* exit the loop, or if the loop
-  // latch was just simplified.
-  if (L->isLoopExiting(OrigLatch) && !SimplifiedLatch)
+  // Rotate only if the loop latch does *not* exit the loop. Before r181230 a
+  // latch that simplifyLoopLatch had just folded into the final exit left the
+  // loop as it was; the PS4 compiler of docs/DECISIONS.md D36 keeps that
+  // behaviour, so SimplifiedLatch is not consulted here.
+  if (L->isLoopExiting(OrigLatch))
     return false;
 
   // Check size of original header and reject loop if it is very big or we can't
